# STUB_HOLD_FILE / STUB_HOLD_TIMEOUT_S (task 007, B.1): the bounded,
# env-driven hold primitive requirement B's race-window conversions (B.2,
# B.3) replace fixed `STUB_SLEEP` padding with -- a test that needs a real
# window to observe an in-flight state (crash mid-write, a resume racing a
# still-running iteration, ...) opens one deterministically instead of
# guessing a sleep long enough to "probably" still be running. Applies to
# EVERY phase, not just Worker, since any role can be the one a test wants
# to catch mid-iteration.
#
# When STUB_HOLD_FILE names a path, this invocation blocks until that path
# exists on disk (the test's own release signal -- typically a plain
# `open(path, "w").close()` or `path.touch()` from the test, once it has
# observed whatever it was waiting to observe) or STUB_HOLD_TIMEOUT_S
# seconds elapse, whichever comes first. The fallback is DELIBERATELY
# small (default 5.0s, never anything close to the iteration timeout): a
# test that forgets to release the hold must fail on this bound, fast,
# rather than hang the test process for the run's full iteration budget.
#
# Reaching the bound is a FAILURE, not a continue: the invocation exits
# with HOLD_TIMEOUT_EXIT_CODE (3) after writing a stderr line naming the
# hold file and the bound, and emits no role payload at all. A test whose
# window never opened therefore goes red on the bound (nonzero stub exit,
# or an engine iteration that failed) instead of passing on a race it
# never actually observed.
#
# The actual wait duration (seconds, monotonic) is always recorded to
# `STUB_HOLD_FILE + ".stub-hold-duration"` -- written whether the hold was
# released or timed out -- so a test can assert its own recorded duration
# stayed within the bound plus a stated margin instead of just trusting
# that the process "returned eventually".
HOLD_TIMEOUT_EXIT_CODE = 3


def _apply_hold():
    hold_file = os.environ.get("STUB_HOLD_FILE")
    if not hold_file:
        return
    timeout_s = float(os.environ.get("STUB_HOLD_TIMEOUT_S", "5"))
    poll_s = float(os.environ.get("STUB_HOLD_POLL_S", "0.02"))
    start = time.monotonic()
    timed_out = False
    while not os.path.exists(hold_file):
        if time.monotonic() - start >= timeout_s:
            timed_out = True
            break
        time.sleep(poll_s)
    duration = time.monotonic() - start
    with open(f"{hold_file}.stub-hold-duration", "w") as _f:
        _f.write(repr(duration))
    if timed_out:
        sys.stderr.write(
            f"stub: hold {hold_file!r} never released; failing on the bounded "
            f"fallback of {timeout_s}s (STUB_HOLD_TIMEOUT_S) after "
            f"{duration:.3f}s\n")
        sys.stderr.flush()
        sys.exit(HOLD_TIMEOUT_EXIT_CODE)


